WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:07.933
- We'll get started.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.130
Good morning.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.920
My name is Lou Tiseo,
I'm the Senior Director

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.312
for Foundational Technologies and ITS.

00:00:17.312 --> 00:00:20.305
I am also co-sponsor with
our chief information

00:00:20.305 --> 00:00:23.850
security officer on what we called

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:27.083
the Next Generation Network
which is this session's about.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.373
I was going through the
website this morning

00:00:30.373 --> 00:00:34.320
and I was looking at
this and I was like, wow.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.450
‘Strengthening Our Network,’
what does that mean?

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.610
And for me it means the foundation,

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:42.320
strengthening the foundation
of our network across campus

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.250
is important to me.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.683
So it was great to see this
theme for this conference.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.450
So the Next Generation Network,
ultimately what is does

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:57.470
is it provides more a
modern, resilient, secure

00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:59.670
network across campus but it has a much,

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:03.703
a bigger reach even outside of campus.

00:01:06.759 --> 00:01:11.759
The network itself is very old,

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.810
and we're seeing today
more and more demands

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.270
on that network to help
advance the research,

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.950
especially research, the clinical care,

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:22.383
and the academic areas.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.424
I was at a conference a few weeks ago

00:01:27.424 --> 00:01:29.880
called the Common Solutions Group.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.360
It was about 20 universities
that attended nationwide.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.390
Out of all the places, it was in Nebraska

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.980
so went to the University of
Nebraska for this conference.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:42.230
And one of the topics there was talking

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:43.723
about campus networks.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.520
There was about 10
universities that were doing

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.440
very similar to what we're
doing, what you're gonna see

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:54.230
about how we're gonna
modernize our network.

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:56.946
Different stages, different areas,

00:01:56.946 --> 00:02:01.840
but the question was really
what's driving the change?

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.840
Security is huge for us, and
I don't mean cybersecurity

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.150
from external attacks.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.362
That's there, we have defenses for that,

00:02:12.362 --> 00:02:14.390
but it's internally as well.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.330
It's when people bring their own devices,

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:19.300
they get on the network, there's
things that are happening.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.860
There's a lot of what we
call east-west traffic

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.990
within the network that
we don't know sometimes

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:28.563
what's happening or even
hard to find where it is.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:35.858
Increased devices, IOT devices,
brings your own devices,

00:02:35.858 --> 00:02:39.850
AV on the network, it
just keeps getting bigger

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:41.980
and more and more demand on the network

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:44.943
from packets to demand to bandwidth.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:47.610
The average student today brings

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:51.140
between six to eight devices to school

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:54.040
that want to attach to the network.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.794
Gaming systems, iPads, phones, iWatches,

00:02:57.794 --> 00:03:01.310
IOT devices, doorbells for their rooms.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:04.643
I mean so it just continues
to grow and then grow.

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.640
Applications moving to the Cloud,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.440
I can personally speak about this

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.565
because I ran the Cloud technologies group

00:03:13.565 --> 00:03:18.565
and the push for size applications,

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.170
Infrastructure as a Service, AWS, Azure,

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.750
Google Platforms, doing
compute out in the Cloud,

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:28.310
big data storage out in the Cloud.

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:31.860
This is taxing our network
going out of the university

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.860
and how do we handle that?

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.270
Higher speeds for research,

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.240
moving data across the university.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.360
We have a high performance
computing center west campus,

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.850
they need to move big
amounts of data over there

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:48.360
for the 40,000 cores that they
have to process that data.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.980
And now we're even
talking about regionally.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:55.027
How do we collaborate with UMass

00:03:55.027 --> 00:03:59.130
and all these other
institutions on the Northeast?

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:03.930
I'm a member of a Northeast
regional network team

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:07.040
that's trying to build
a much faster network

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.450
throughout the northeast.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:11.020
There's one on the west
coast it's called Quilt,

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:12.250
it's very similar.

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:15.613
But these are big demands,
especially in research.

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:18.820
Bring your own devices,
I talked about that,

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.430
just attaching a Wi-Fi.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:25.430
We can have an average of about
30,000 people on the network

00:04:26.452 --> 00:04:30.193
in a peak day which is quite
big, just for this campus.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:35.270
What I talk about too
is the technical debt

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:37.710
and a lot of universities are seeing this,

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.470
you know the investment in the network

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:42.230
was never really there
over the last 10 years,

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:45.770
however the network technology
has taken leaps and bounds

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:48.130
in the last 10 years of the technology

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:52.190
from software defined networking
to software defined access,

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:55.950
to perimeters that we
put in and being able

00:04:55.950 --> 00:05:00.020
to machine learn devices
that come on the network

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:01.563
and how do we deal with it?

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:07.360
I talked a little bit about
the growing dependencies.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:11.065
So for Yale, academic delivery, right?

00:05:11.065 --> 00:05:12.663
Books online.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.080
I have a son that's going
to college next year

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.900
and they're actually now
selling pages online,

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:23.310
pages of a book that you
can buy for a week, right?

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:24.960
On a specific topic.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.010
So you know the demand more
for video and stuff like that

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:33.010
in the academic delivery is
getting bigger and bigger.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:34.410
Research, I talked about that.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:36.890
It's just very overwhelming
for me even just

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:40.080
to figure out how this data gets moved.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.090
We're actually talking about
how do we actually move

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:45.967
the compute to the data using
what we call containerization.

00:05:45.967 --> 00:05:47.830
You know, we built containers here,

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:51.200
and I'm gonna move those
containers to UMass

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.660
and let them process on their data.

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:56.332
So the collaboration with
the other universities is big

00:05:56.332 --> 00:05:58.520
and just even on campus.

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:00.447
We have what we call the science network

00:06:00.447 --> 00:06:02.113
and the science network
is a physical network,

00:06:02.113 --> 00:06:04.590
it's a 100 gig network but it's only

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:07.340
in specific buildings that are needed.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:09.080
Like, there's no science
network in this building

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.420
but there is one getting ready

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:15.240
for the big science
building that's coming up.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.540
Clinical care as many of
you know, I mean you know

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:21.988
the call centers today we run call centers

00:06:21.988 --> 00:06:25.002
on the Yale School of Medicine side.

00:06:25.002 --> 00:06:28.430
You call them, everything's
on the computer.

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:29.404
They know who you are,

00:06:29.404 --> 00:06:31.410
they know what apartments'
you're gonna have,

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:32.910
what your test results look like,

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:34.540
things like that to help you through

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:36.903
and even to the point where these doctors

00:06:36.903 --> 00:06:40.950
are in the Yale Health Plan
where they have carts and stuff

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.580
that need to connect to the
network, things like that.

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:45.603
So no more paper anymore.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.290
We were just talking the other
day about Milford Hospital

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:50.820
we all know New Haven Health Services

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:51.800
borrowed Milford Hospital.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:54.890
Well they were never computerized

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:59.100
to the point where we are today.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:01.050
I mean, they have basic computerization,

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:03.380
but a lot of their records
were, are manual records

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:06.180
which is, they're gonna be
spending quite a bit of money

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:09.160
on getting Milford
Hospital up and running.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:12.450
And of course the cultural heritage side

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:17.143
for worldwide scholars.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:22.460
The biggest challenge for us in this area

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:27.040
is that digital born
collections are very important

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:29.520
and how do you preserve those?

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.090
Where do you preserve those?

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:32.888
You know, you have to save those

00:07:32.888 --> 00:07:37.750
in like eight different places
around the country sometimes.

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:41.160
So I'm working actually very closely

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.486
with the library on trying to define

00:07:44.486 --> 00:07:49.060
do we need a cultural heritage
digital collection type

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:51.880
of network built into our architecture

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:54.218
and what does that mean for the museums

00:07:54.218 --> 00:07:57.033
as well as the libraries
that are tied to it?

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:00.993
Very simple.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:05.080
We have a very aging network, very old,

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:07.970
mixture of many, many
different technologies.

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:10.270
It's a complete comprehensive replacement

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:12.160
of the hardware throughout the network

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.460
and I'll show you the
numbers what that means.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:22.200
The security side of this is
gonna built into the network.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.130
It's a different type of model

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:27.210
on how we actually
intercept different things

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:28.970
that are happening on the network,

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:33.430
how we can pick up a
device that's across campus

00:08:33.430 --> 00:08:35.330
and say oh, that's playing really badly.

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:37.750
I just wanna put it over here.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:41.690
Put him in a different
network that's kinda safe.

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:46.580
Where today, a lot of times
you'll see part of my team

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:47.770
running out of the building trying

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:49.450
to go find that in the building, right?

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:52.893
So it's very, very manual process.

00:08:54.250 --> 00:08:55.800
And we'll talk about
the number of networks.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:00.210
So the key thing really
for me is the reliability,

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:03.293
the resiliency, the
visibility of this network.

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:10.410
Pictures tell a thousand words here,

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:12.190
and this is not the worst
one, I'll just tell you.

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:15.210
This, you know that
pipe I hit my head on it

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:16.540
when I went to go see it.

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:21.540
So you know we have the
physical state of the network,

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:24.550
just even the closets
that are around campus

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:26.380
are in really poor shape.

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:27.990
They're not managed properly.

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:30.420
There's a lot of work
that needs to be done

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:32.290
just in the conditioning of some

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:35.020
of these old buildings that we have to do.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:37.900
And the project itself,
you know I'll give you

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:41.200
a little preface on how
this project happened.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:43.400
You know, it's a five year project

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:48.860
and I spent probably eight months working

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:50.830
with the budget advisory groups,

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:53.540
the financial committees,
the business office,

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:55.540
all the way up to the board of directors

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:00.670
on presentations for this and
we've been fully approved.

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:02.280
Not committed, but fully approved

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:04.920
to spend $50 million to
replace this network.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:06.193
So it's a big number.

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:13.820
As you can tell here, 55% of the equipment

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:16.813
is beyond its recommended service age.

00:10:18.628 --> 00:10:21.616
The segmentations are a concern

00:10:21.616 --> 00:10:25.163
to the security side of things.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:28.960
Just standards in general,
I mean we probably have

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:30.891
about four different
types of manufacturers

00:10:30.891 --> 00:10:35.153
of switching equipment and Wi-Fi points.

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:39.650
So here are the key deliverables.

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:42.353
So we need to remediate about 1,400 rooms,

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:47.840
closets that connect all
the floors in one building.

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:52.500
So this could range from a
complete redo to the room

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.700
to just wiring, to just
even securing the room.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:57.850
We have rooms where
they're just in closets.

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:00.013
We have a room that's in,

00:11:00.013 --> 00:11:04.120
to get to it you have to go
through the women's restroom.

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:07.490
You know, so it's very
difficult to even keep control

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:10.700
of these rooms just from
a physical standpoint.

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:12.880
Some of the them don't
have key, locks on it,

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:14.440
or proxy locks on it.

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:17.010
We find people put things
in it like a microwave

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:18.501
in a room or something like that,

00:11:18.501 --> 00:11:21.227
so we have to take better
control of these rooms.

00:11:21.227 --> 00:11:24.100
And you know everything
from even air conditioning,

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:28.100
and power, and fiber
connections to specific rooms.

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:30.130
So it is a very lengthy process here

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:34.280
a long assessment that
has to go on with this

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:36.640
but it's gonna go on
for the next five years,

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:39.460
just assessing and remediating the rooms.

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:41.930
We're replacing about 9,000 devices,

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:44.023
that includes Wi-Fi devices as well.

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:49.364
The Wi-Fi devices, we have
actually a big investment

00:11:49.364 --> 00:11:53.140
in newer Wi-Fi devices
so we'll let them age out

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:57.623
and replace them as they
get replaced over time.

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:00.960
Consolidating five networks,

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:02.720
and we're focused on five networks only

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:04.570
because those are the
five biggest networks.

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:06.900
We have many other physical networks,

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:10.320
and these are physical so
if you go into a closet,

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:15.100
you will see a rack of switches
three times, let's say,

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:17.590
one for a facilities network,

00:12:17.590 --> 00:12:20.760
one for a public safety network,
one for a campus network.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:22.990
That's gonna come together
through software defined

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:26.113
networking right, into one architecture.

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:32.518
Role-based access, you'll
see in the back part

00:12:32.518 --> 00:12:34.562
as we go through the presentation

00:12:34.562 --> 00:12:39.562
that role-based access
is defining who you are

00:12:39.641 --> 00:12:41.990
and what you get on the network.

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:46.260
There's researchers who are
gonna need 100 gig connections

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:48.530
and we can define that for a researcher,

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:51.050
where you can have the
administrative offices

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:54.680
just needs access and
maybe needs like you know

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:58.910
a five gig connection versus
a hundred gig connection.

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:01.750
Who you are, where you are is gonna define

00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:02.583
your different roles.

00:13:02.583 --> 00:13:06.680
If I'm a clinician, I wanna
be able to move from here

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:09.140
to Yale New Haven Health
and be able to use

00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:11.740
my same ID and not have multiple IDs.

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:15.750
That's the role-based access piece of it.

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:18.870
And automating cybersecurity responses,

00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:21.020
I spoke a little bit
about it but it's more

00:13:22.432 --> 00:13:27.313
machine-based responsing
versus manual responsing.

00:13:31.774 --> 00:13:34.280
So I talked a little bit
about these in the last slide

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:37.170
but a seamless user experience

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:40.300
no matter what profile you're in,

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:44.250
if you're just a student
where you deal with LORIS data

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:48.720
or if you're a clinician
who deals with HIPAA

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:52.420
or a researcher that deals
with different other,

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:55.943
like FERPA or ePHI data.

00:13:56.960 --> 00:13:58.470
You know it's gonna be seamless.

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:00.873
You go here, you go to
a different network,

00:14:02.250 --> 00:14:03.900
you know over to the Med Campus

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:05.810
which is a different network,

00:14:05.810 --> 00:14:08.030
and you'll have that seamless connection

00:14:08.030 --> 00:14:09.593
to all your resources,

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:13.630
even if you're VPN in,
it'll be the same thing.

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:18.303
You'll just be looking like a
remote site onto the network.

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:22.690
Faster speeds are gonna
go 10 to 40 times faster.

00:14:22.690 --> 00:14:26.040
I mean, really the network
it's not the fiber,

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:27.410
it's not the cabling in the network,

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:29.050
it's the switching, right?

00:14:29.050 --> 00:14:32.230
So we change these switches
to a much more robust

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:35.013
and faster switching technology.

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:37.563
Fewer network disruptions.

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:42.400
I've been in this position now
for a little bit over a year.

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:46.090
I made it very visible on weekly updates

00:14:46.090 --> 00:14:48.493
about outages that are
happening around campus.

00:14:48.493 --> 00:14:50.173
It's all the way to the provost.

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:52.940
You know, this week we had eight outages,

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:57.940
three buildings down, and
it was important to do that

00:14:58.250 --> 00:14:59.930
because we never did that before

00:14:59.930 --> 00:15:02.830
and giving that visibility really shows

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:05.493
the concern that we've had.

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:12.410
Machine speed detection
for breaches again,

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:16.970
built into the network there's
this security defined access

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:20.260
and they call ICE which
is another piece of it

00:15:21.390 --> 00:15:23.540
and DNA Center which
is another piece of it

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:28.130
that is going to help
with machine detection

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:31.120
and visibility of where
things are happening.

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:33.130
Like I said, it's very,
very difficult today

00:15:33.130 --> 00:15:35.253
to actually find things.

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:40.923
Here's a very high level schedule.

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:45.080
We spent a good part of FY19 really,

00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:48.428
from a programmatic
perspective, building a team.

00:15:48.428 --> 00:15:52.705
We are moving to all Cisco technology.

00:15:52.705 --> 00:15:55.100
I had got a lot of questions about that.

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:59.660
We actually have Cisco here on
site for the next two years,

00:15:59.660 --> 00:16:01.880
their advanced services
in engineering's teams

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:03.480
are here working with the teams.

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:09.490
We have a number of different
providers that work with Cisco

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:12.870
that are here to help us with
deployment and architecture.

00:16:12.870 --> 00:16:15.440
So we spent this year
really programmatically

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:17.915
bringing the team together,
understanding what it means,

00:16:17.915 --> 00:16:22.300
and working on the design
and architecture piece

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:23.620
of this program.

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:27.005
We're starting with some pilot buildings,

00:16:27.005 --> 00:16:29.830
probably I think soon,
very soon, this summer.

00:16:29.830 --> 00:16:33.120
25 Science Park is gonna be the first one,

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:36.297
'cause we're gonna eat our own
dog food and learn from that.

00:16:36.297 --> 00:16:39.015
But we're taking two
administrative buildings

00:16:39.015 --> 00:16:43.690
on Central Campus here
and then there's been

00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:45.990
a little bit of a turn of the tides

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:47.900
because of some recent
things that have happened

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:51.540
with the Med School Campus network,

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:55.280
but we are accelerating
deployment on the Med School side

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:58.803
to correct some major issues there.

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:04.740
So over the next, once we
get through the pilot cycle,

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:07.562
I think once we get through FY20,

00:17:07.562 --> 00:17:11.223
we'll have a whole
deployment plan ready to go

00:17:11.223 --> 00:17:14.990
for the next few years
and it's just really going

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:16.200
through each of these buildings.

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:18.330
Up front of this there's a couple

00:17:18.330 --> 00:17:20.530
of other work streams like I mentioned.

00:17:20.530 --> 00:17:22.880
We're looking at the whole
fiber plant on the university,

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:27.220
making sure we have diverse connections,

00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:30.117
and, did I shut this off?

00:17:34.150 --> 00:17:34.983
We're going out.

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:36.973
I'll speak louder.

00:17:39.545 --> 00:17:41.570
It looks like it went out.

00:17:41.570 --> 00:17:45.260
So we're looking at the rooms and stuff,

00:17:45.260 --> 00:17:47.760
so we're trying to get
that what we call layer one

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:52.420
set in place before we start
putting in the electronics.

00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:53.253
Okay?

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:59.310
So, we're defining this
in two different releases.

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:01.384
The foundational release,
which I mentioned before

00:18:01.384 --> 00:18:05.280
is focused on resiliency, reliability,

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:10.280
stability, visibility, and
hopefully when we do that phase,

00:18:11.930 --> 00:18:14.396
my emails each week to the provost

00:18:14.396 --> 00:18:17.940
and the down buildings will
reduce down to nothing,

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:21.453
'cause that's really the
primary focus of this.

00:18:22.550 --> 00:18:26.840
And then moving into the
advanced security side of things,

00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:30.100
we have a lab setup now at Science Park

00:18:31.910 --> 00:18:34.910
and you know we're gonna
have teams of people,

00:18:34.910 --> 00:18:38.163
public safety coming in
trying all their devices.

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:41.240
Facilities has an enormous amount

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:43.060
of devices just to run a building.

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:44.860
They need to test all those in the lab

00:18:44.860 --> 00:18:49.163
before we roll out and config those.

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:53.160
When you plug something into the network,

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:55.470
if it is not defined
through a configuration,

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:57.020
it will not allowed on the network.

00:18:57.020 --> 00:18:59.910
It will be put in a space that has

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:03.880
to have a config made for it, type, right?

00:19:03.880 --> 00:19:06.950
So we're hoping that
over time that that will

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:11.450
that will be easily done
to allow those devices,

00:19:11.450 --> 00:19:14.340
but we do get devices
plugged into the network

00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:16.220
that spew a lot of different traffic

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:20.183
that hurts our network today.

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:27.360
So where are we?

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:28.810
I spoke a little bit about this.

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:31.533
Designs are 80% complete.

00:19:31.533 --> 00:19:36.199
Our teams are still
building but the core team

00:19:36.199 --> 00:19:41.040
is been working the last year
on the implementation side

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:45.370
of things as well as
for the pilot buildings,

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:48.720
and then looking ahead for what

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:50.383
the next couple years look like.

00:19:52.070 --> 00:19:55.960
What's next is again the
detail migrations plans.

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:59.280
We're gonna focus out on
West Campus in the beginning

00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:01.668
probably as the normal project begins,

00:20:01.668 --> 00:20:04.820
only because we have a
little more flexibility

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:09.820
with the layer one piece of West Campus,

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:14.103
so from a physical plant standpoint.

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:17.720
Use cases, we need to build use cases.

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:20.330
That's one of our next steps as well.

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:22.600
Meeting with the research community,

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:24.820
meeting with the clinical community,

00:20:24.820 --> 00:20:27.510
meeting with the facilities community,

00:20:27.510 --> 00:20:30.803
and understand all the use
cases that need to happen

00:20:30.803 --> 00:20:34.943
for those areas and
also the academic side.

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:41.050
That's all I have.

